+
+reaches() soundness (r2, install-script unwiring)
+-------------------------------------------------
+The legacy ``delivery.entrypoint_invoke`` fallback then overrode that sound
+verdict with two GENERIC markers: for a ``files:`` entry it took the bare
+artifact filename (``install.sh`` — the name every install-script patch
+ships, so any compose that runs ANY install script matched) and, for a
+directory entry, ``Path(rel).parent.name`` (always ``patches`` — so any
+compose that mounts ANY ``patches/`` dir matched). The fallback therefore
+answered True unconditionally for those entries, and a compose that had
+been unwired from an install-script patch (mount + invoke removed) still
+reported as reached.
+
+Two fixes, in ``reaches``:
+
+* the fallback is now SUBORDINATE to the spec: a patch whose
+  ``delivery_spec`` fully describes the wiring it claims
+  (:func:`_spec_is_authoritative`) gets no fallback at all, so a stray
+  non-mount mention of the patch dir (an echo in a bootstrap script, a
+  README pointer) can no longer resurrect a compose the spec says is
+  unwired;
+* where the fallback does run (specs that leave an insertion point
+  undescribed) it probes only identity-bearing basenames — this patch's
+  own directory name and its non-generic artifacts — never ``patches``
+  or ``install.sh`` (:func:`_path_identity_markers`).
+
+And the spec's own mount check is now source-attributed
+(:func:`_mount_source_present`): a mount line counts only when the declared
+in-container target AND one of this patch's own source-side basenames
+appear on the SAME line. Two patches can share a target
+(``qwen-w4a8-int8-act`` and ``qwen38-sglang-autoround-w4a8-v0519`` both
+land on ``/etc/club3090/w4a8``), so a target-only match would let each
+claim the other engine's compose.
